  "mach:judgement": "Assess",
  "mach:judgementReason": "A timely, fully open (MIT) benchmark for a safety problem that matters for ambient clinical scribes: whether AI-written SOAP notes drop safety-critical facts or fabricate them. Its design is sound and reproducible — 300 synthetic primary-care dialogues, 4,800 published before/after notes, scoring scripts, and a cross-family 4-verifier LLM judge panel to reduce single-model bias — and its central, well-quantified finding (omissions ~43× more common than hallucinations) is a genuinely useful contribution to clinical-documentation safety. It is cataloged as an open benchmark, not an endorsement of the proprietary models it tests. Marked Assess rather than Adopt because: it is a recent v2 release from a single vendor (Omi Health) with no independent reproduction yet; the corpus is synthetic, so findings need confirmation on real clinical transcripts; and the evaluation is partly self-referential (it also showcases the vendor's own 'Omi Guard' layer). Valuable now for teams building or vetting clinical-note generation who need a public safety harness. Companion speech-to-text model cataloged separately as omi-med-stt (ai-ml-models).",
  "mach:editorialReviewedAt": "2026-06-24",
